(BUGS_FOUND #84: `aren't` is the other contraction LANGUAGE.md's Logical
 Operators table documents. It means `are not`, so it lexes as two tokens
 and reads in the multi-subject comparison exactly where `are not` does.
 Each pair prints the same line twice.)

a boolean called p is true.
a boolean called q is false.

If p, and q aren't false then, print "neither is false". Otherwise, print "one is false".
If p, and q are not false then, print "neither is false". Otherwise, print "one is false".

a number called x is 4.
a number called y is 4.

If x, and y aren't 9 then, print "neither is nine". Otherwise, print "one is nine".
If x, and y are not 9 then, print "neither is nine". Otherwise, print "one is nine".

(Case is not significant here either)
If x, and y AREN'T 4 then, print "neither is four". Otherwise, print "one is four".
